  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES: \n\nLATE SATURDAY AFTERNOON--SATURDAY NIGHT...A potent shortwave and \nassociated strong cold front is forecast to progress southeast \nacross the region, supporting an area of rain impacting much of \ncentral and eastern Kansas, although likely most widespread over \ngenerally the northeast quarter of the state. The latest guidance is \na bit more progressive than the NBM, so later shifts may need to \nincrease PoPs a bit more mid-late afternoon Saturday. Also, we ended \nup increasing QPF amounts over NBM, with a 50/50 blend of WPC/NBM \ngiven model consensus. If trends continue, later shifts will need to \nincrease QPF amounts more, with the latest guidance supporting up to \naround 0.25-0.50 inches for northeast portions of the forecast area \n(along/northeast of a line extending from Lincoln to Marion to \nEureka to Chanute).  \n\nAlso, on the back side of the departing precipitation shield \nSaturday night, thermal profiles may support a brief mix of wet snow \nas colder air advects southeast. Surface wet-bulbs colder than the \nmid-30s are unlikely, so no impacts or accumulations are expected. \n\nMID-LATE WEEK...The next chance for meaningful rainfall looks to \narrive sometime mid-late next week, as deterministic and ensemble \nconsensus progresses another potent shortwave over Mid-America. Run-\nto-run and model-to-model consistency remains fairly low this far \nout, so uncertainty is high regarding timing, location, and \npotential rainfall amounts. Stay tuned as we refine forecast details \nin the coming days.\n\nTEMPERATURES & WIND:\n\nStout/gusty northwest winds are expected to expand southeast across \ncentral and eastern Kansas late Saturday afternoon through Saturday \nnight, in wake of a strong cold front. Per model soundings, 40-50+ \nmph wind gusts look probable, strongest during the evening \nimmediately in wake of the progressing cold front. A wind advisory \nmay eventually be needed.\n\nMostly seasonably mild temperatures are expected the next several \ndays, although modest cool downs are likely Sunday and again late \nnext week and next weekend in wake of separate cold fronts. The \nwarmest days look to be Tuesday--Thursday, with some 60s likely, as \nheights rise and southerly flow increases ahead of late next week's \nsystem.",
